WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:06.000
A brand new space pirate character has beef with&nbsp;
Greef in the latest episode of "The Mandalorian,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:11.160
but the man behind the mask is an industry&nbsp;
veteran. Here's what Vane looks like IRL.

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:14.640
Season 3 of "The Mandalorian"&nbsp;
offers up another glimpse into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:18.960
the lives of Pedro Pascal's Din&nbsp;
Djarin, the ever-adorable Grogu,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:24.120
and plenty of other returning characters, but&nbsp;
it also adds quite a few fresh faces to the mix.

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:29.880
Among the newcomers is an intimidating space&nbsp;
pirate named Vane. A frequenter of the Nevarro&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:33.900
locale, Vane doesn't take kindly to the new&nbsp;
direction of the city after its liberation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:38.280
from the hands of the Empire. He's also&nbsp;
clearly got a history with Greef Karga,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:41.943
a plot thread that seems ripe for&nbsp;
expansion in the series' future.

00:00:41.943 --> 00:00:46.560
It was your cut of my boss'&nbsp;
treasure that built this saloon."

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:51.660
Beyond his plot significance, the most striking&nbsp;
thing about Vane is his physical appearance. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:56.460
space pirate's face is quite striking, lacking&nbsp;
any discernible nose and sporting horns not too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.460 --> 00:01:01.320
dissimilar from those on Darth Maul. It's such an&nbsp;
out-there appearance that viewers may be shocked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:06.195
to learn what the actor behind Vane looks like&nbsp;
when he's not buried under heaps of prosthetics.

00:01:06.195 --> 00:01:08.820
The man who plays Vane is Marti Matulis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:12.660
a 6-foot-4 American actor known&nbsp;
for his creature work in horror,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:18.360
fantasy and science fiction. Matulis has appeared&nbsp;
in a bunch of popular TV shows for decades, though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:22.946
almost invariably as characters that require&nbsp;
heavy prosthetics or elaborate costume design.

00:01:22.946 --> 00:01:27.120
Back in the sixth season of "American&nbsp;
Horror Story," Matulis recurred as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:30.420
sinister Piggy Man — a ghost that&nbsp;
sports the giant severed head of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:34.440
a pig. He appeared as several different&nbsp;
monstrous characters on "Teen Wolf," and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:38.520
he's occupied a similar role for the&nbsp;
Paramount+ supernatural drama "Evil."

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:41.820
One of the actor's most high-profile&nbsp;
appearances in recent years, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:46.080
is his role in the 2022 horror film&nbsp;
"Smile." In the movie's climax,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:51.120
when audiences finally get a brief glimpse at the&nbsp;
true form of the malevolent Smile Entity haunting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:55.680
the characters, Matulis is the one who brings&nbsp;
the demonic mass of flesh and teeth to life.

00:01:55.680 --> 00:02:00.540
All in all, Matulis is quietly one of the key&nbsp;
players in the modern landscape of horror and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:04.680
sci-fi. If anything, his eventual debut&nbsp;
into the canon of "Star Wars" through "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:09.480
Mandalorian" was something of an inevitability.&nbsp;
It also means he gets to join the distinguished&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:14.339
club of actors who have appeared in both "Star&nbsp;
Trek" and "Star Wars" at one point or another.

00:02:14.339 --> 00:02:17.640
On an episode of Daniel Scherl's&nbsp;
"Memories of a Moonbird" podcast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:22.421
Matulis went in-depth on how demanding roles&nbsp;
that require heavy prosthetics can get,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.421 --> 00:02:27.060
telling the host that he typically sports a shaved&nbsp;
head and face, to make for easier gluing. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:31.707
transformation often takes hours to complete,&nbsp;
and Matulis, of course, has a front row seat.

00:02:31.707 --> 00:02:37.140
"I just sit in a chair looking into a&nbsp;
mirror as I watch these ridiculously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:40.500
talented people build this creation on top of me."

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:43.980
Even once the makeup and materials&nbsp;
have been applied, Matulis has to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:47.760
account for his altered physicality when&nbsp;
giving his performance. He told Scherl,

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:51.960
"I might have to put 30 percent more effort&nbsp;
into my angry face or whatever happens during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:55.680
the scene. So, that's my time to figure&nbsp;
out how I can translate myself through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:59.078
the makeup and make the makeup look&nbsp;
alive and not like a rubber mask."

00:02:59.078 --> 00:03:04.260
It's tough work, but Matulis has nevertheless&nbsp;
stuck with the job for more than 20 years. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:08.460
major roles like Vane in "The Mandalorian"&nbsp;
and the Smile Entity in "Smile" demonstrate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:12.000
that commitment has paid off.&nbsp;
Matulis shared on the podcast,

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:15.360
"As a younger actor, I thought, 'Oh,&nbsp;
that's really cool but it's more of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:20.509
step to do something else.' Now, as a nearly&nbsp;
50-year-old kid, I adore this process."

